WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.280
Hi, I'm Hap.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.840
Today we're looking at package.json,

3
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:12.840
a file npm needs before it can
do anything for your project.

4
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.160
Everything I'm about to run happens

5
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.600
in the terminal inside VS Code,
so let me open that first.

6
00:00:20.645 --> 00:00:22.325
That's the terminal every set

7
00:00:22.325 --> 00:00:25.605
of instructions means when it
tells you to run a command.

8
00:00:26.085 --> 00:00:30.725
The one I'm running now is npm
init, with a -y on the end,

9
00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:33.285
and it makes the file this
whole video is about.

10
00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:36.635
There it is,

11
00:00:36.955 --> 00:00:39.755
package.json, and it's
an ordinary text file.

12
00:00:40.395 --> 00:00:43.195
You can open it, read
it, and type into it,

13
00:00:43.195 --> 00:00:45.595
the same as any other
file in your project.

14
00:00:46.155 --> 00:00:47.195
Where

15
00:00:47.195 --> 00:00:49.995
package.json sits defines
a project's root folder,

16
00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.470
and where this root is needs to be right.

17
00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.310
Web developers need the root

18
00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:57.190
to be in the repository's
home, the same place where

19
00:00:58.150 --> 00:00:59.350
README.md is.

20
00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.870
Now look at what npm actually wrote.

21
00:01:03.475 --> 00:01:06.675
There's a name, taken from
the folder you are in.

22
00:01:06.995 --> 00:01:10.275
A version, starting at 1.0.0.

23
00:01:10.515 --> 00:01:14.035
A description, which npm went
and read out of your README.

24
00:01:14.275 --> 00:01:18.835
An author, filled in if you've told npm
who you are, and empty if you haven't.

25
00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.580
And a few more: main,
keywords, type, and scripts.

26
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.460
The -y is what skipped the questions
npm would otherwise have asked,

27
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.620
and it answered every one
of them with a default.

28
00:01:32.855 --> 00:01:35.895
The install command adds
something to that file,

29
00:01:35.975 --> 00:01:38.375
and it does it without you typing into it.

30
00:01:38.615 --> 00:01:44.615
I'm installing a package called
cowsay, with --save-dev on the end.

31
00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.780
When you run npm install with a package
name, npm goes to the registry,

32
00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.660
which is an online library of packages,
and fetches the one you asked for.

33
00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:59.260
Installing is not the same as
downloading a single file.

34
00:02:00.015 --> 00:02:04.655
Instead it brings the package and
everything that package itself depends on.

35
00:02:06.415 --> 00:02:07.615
Watch the bottom of

36
00:02:08.015 --> 00:02:09.135
package.json.

37
00:02:09.135 --> 00:02:11.615
There's a new key called devDependencies,

38
00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.130
holding the name of what I
installed and a version.

39
00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:19.530
That dev part comes from the special
flag I used when I installed:

40
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:23.050
it marks cowsay as something
you use while you work,

41
00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:26.250
rather than something the finished
site needs in order to run.

42
00:02:27.265 --> 00:02:29.665
npm edited your file for you.

43
00:02:29.985 --> 00:02:34.065
Two other things appeared beside
it: a folder called node_modules,

44
00:02:34.065 --> 00:02:36.945
holding the actual code, and a file called

45
00:02:38.385 --> 00:02:41.265
package-lock.json, which records
the exact versions that landed.

46
00:02:41.852 --> 00:02:45.052
That one is generated, and
you never edit it by hand.

47
00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:49.850
Everything you've been looking at is JSON,

48
00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.570
and JSON is stricter
than you might expect.

49
00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:58.410
Every key and every text value is wrapped
in double quotes, never single ones.

50
00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:01.210
A colon separates a name from its value.

51
00:03:01.945 --> 00:03:06.025
In a JSON list a comma separates
one pair from the next,

52
00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.665
and there is no comma after the last one.

53
00:03:09.225 --> 00:03:11.705
That's the whole set of
rules you need here,

54
00:03:11.945 --> 00:03:17.890
and getting one of them wrong is
the difference between a file npm
can read and a file it refuses.

55
00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:22.690
One thing that trips people
up in the other direction:

56
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.730
the order of the keys means nothing.

57
00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:29.490
npm doesn't care whether
name comes before version.

58
00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:31.810
What it cares about is the commas.

59
00:03:33.912 --> 00:03:35.272
Look at scripts.

60
00:03:35.592 --> 00:03:38.792
It is a JSON list
enclosed in curly braces,

61
00:03:38.872 --> 00:03:43.592
and it already has something in it: a
test entry that npm wrote as an example.

62
00:03:43.912 --> 00:03:46.952
Having a line there matters
because when you're told to add

63
00:03:46.952 --> 00:03:51.197
a script to package.json, you're
adding a line to that list.

64
00:03:51.357 --> 00:03:54.477
In JSON lists you must
make sure each entry

65
00:03:54.477 --> 00:03:57.197
ends with a comma except for the last one.

66
00:03:59.037 --> 00:04:00.237
Watch me do it.

67
00:04:00.237 --> 00:04:04.622
I'm calling mine say, and the
command it runs is cowsay,

68
00:04:04.782 --> 00:04:06.942
followed by the phrase I want back.

69
00:04:07.502 --> 00:04:11.422
The name on the left is mine, and
I could have called it anything.

70
00:04:11.822 --> 00:04:14.782
The part on the right is the
command that actually runs.

71
00:04:16.933 --> 00:04:19.973
There's one step between
typing that and it working,

72
00:04:20.213 --> 00:04:23.733
and it catches people who are
otherwise doing everything right.

73
00:04:25.573 --> 00:04:26.773
npm reads

74
00:04:27.253 --> 00:04:28.453
package.json off the disk.

75
00:04:29.318 --> 00:04:32.278
If your editor still
shows an unsaved file,

76
00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:35.958
the script you just typed exists
on your screen and nowhere else.

77
00:04:36.358 --> 00:04:40.678
Be sure to save it first before testing
to see if the new script runs correctly.

78
00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:44.279
Now the payoff.

79
00:04:44.599 --> 00:04:45.639
You run

80
00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:48.759
npm run, and then the name
you gave your script.

81
00:04:49.159 --> 00:04:51.559
The first two lines you'll
see both start with

82
00:04:52.119 --> 00:04:53.399
npm notice run.

83
00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:56.279
There is one naming the
package and the script,

84
00:04:56.354 --> 00:04:59.074
and one naming the command
it's about to run.

85
00:04:59.314 --> 00:05:03.154
That isn't an error and it isn't
your output printed twice.

86
00:05:03.394 --> 00:05:06.354
It's npm telling you
exactly what it found in

87
00:05:07.074 --> 00:05:08.674
package.json and what it's running.

88
00:05:09.462 --> 00:05:12.982
Everything after those two lines
is your command doing its work.

89
00:05:13.945 --> 00:05:15.145
So that's

90
00:05:15.145 --> 00:05:15.945
package.json.

91
00:05:15.945 --> 00:05:20.745
npm made it, npm added to it, you
edited it, and npm read it back.

92
00:05:20.905 --> 00:05:23.785
Every project you build from
here starts with this file.
